export const meta = {
  name: 'fringe-ticketing-research',
  description: 'Research who sells Fringe tickets, the definitive provider list, and listings/affiliate APIs',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Recon', detail: 'parallel web + local-catalogue research across 6 topics' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'adversarial fact-check of each topic\'s load-bearing claims' },
  ],
}

const CTX = `CONTEXT (shared by all researchers):
You are researching for EdFringeNow, a client-side web product for the Edinburgh Festival Fringe (discovery + trip planner). The repo currently gets its show catalogue by scraping edfringe.com's GraphQL API (Tikketr platform, edfringe-tikketr-web-api.equhost.com) nightly via GitHub Actions. The owner wants to understand how Fringe tickets are ACTUALLY sold, who all the "event providers" (venue operators) are, whether a third party can sell tickets itself (affiliate/agent/API), and whether provider or aggregator listings APIs could replace the edfringe scrape.

TODAY is 2026-07-31. The 2026 Fringe runs 7-31 August (3,649 shows, 258 venues). Prefer 2025/2026 sources; older sources are fine for structural facts if you say so.

TOOLING CONSTRAINTS: You have WebSearch and WebFetch. edfringe.com returns 403 to WebFetch and equhost.com is blocked by the egress proxy - for those domains rely on WebSearch result snippets and third-party coverage. Most other sites (venue operators, news, wikis) are fetchable; if a fetch fails, fall back to search. Cite EVERY fact with a real URL you actually saw.

OUTPUT: your final output is consumed by a script, not a human. Fill the schema honestly: mark confidence low when a claim rests on one thin source; put what you could NOT establish into openQuestions rather than guessing. keyClaims = the 2-3 claims your topic's conclusions rest on (the ones that, if wrong, change the product decision).`

const FINDINGS = {
  type: 'object',
  required: ['summary', 'findings', 'keyClaims', 'openQuestions', 'sources'],
  properties: {
    summary: { type: 'string', description: '5-10 sentence answer to the topic questions' },
    findings: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', required: ['fact', 'source', 'confidence'], properties: {
      fact: { type: 'string' }, source: { type: 'string', description: 'URL' },
      confidence: { type: 'string', enum: ['high', 'medium', 'low'] } } } },
    keyClaims: { type: 'array', maxItems: 3, items: { type: 'string' } },
    openQuestions: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    sources: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', required: ['title', 'url'], properties: {
      title: { type: 'string' }, url: { type: 'string' } } } },
  },
}

const OPERATORS = {
  type: 'object',
  required: ['operators', 'totalShows', 'totalVenues', 'unmatchedVenues', 'method'],
  properties: {
    operators: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', required: ['operator', 'venues', 'shows'], properties: {
      operator: { type: 'string' }, venues: { type: 'number' }, shows: { type: 'number' },
      exampleVenues: { type: 'array', maxItems: 5, items: { type: 'string' } } } } },
    totalShows: { type: 'number' },
    totalVenues: { type: 'number' },
    freeShows: { type: 'number', description: 'count of shows with free=true' },
    unmatchedVenues: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'venue names not matched to any operator family (the tail), with show counts in the string' },
    method: { type: 'string', description: 'how grouping was done, and caveats' },
  },
}

const VERDICTS = {
  type: 'object',
  required: ['verdicts'],
  properties: {
    verdicts: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', required: ['claim', 'verdict', 'why'], properties: {
      claim: { type: 'string' },
      verdict: { type: 'string', enum: ['confirmed', 'plausible', 'refuted', 'unverifiable'] },
      why: { type: 'string', description: 'what independent evidence you found, with URLs' },
      correction: { type: 'string', description: 'if refuted or partially wrong: the corrected statement' } } } },
  },
}

const RECON = [
  {
    key: 'architecture', verify: true,
    prompt: `${CTX}

TOPIC: How Fringe ticket purchasing actually works - the institutional architecture.

Questions to answer, each cited:
1. Who runs edfringe.com and its box office? (The Edinburgh Festival Fringe Society - what is it legally: charity? membership org? Is it owned/controlled by venue operators, or independent of them?) How does a show get into the programme (open-access registration - anyone who registers is in)?
2. The allocation model: venues/producers give the central Fringe box office an ALLOCATION of tickets to sell, and sell the remainder through their own box offices. Find published descriptions (Fringe Society pages via search snippets, venue FAQs, participant/artist guidance, press). Our scraped data has a per-performance status NO_ALLOCATION_CONTACT_VENUE - evidence of what that means in this model.
3. What platform powers edfringe.com ticketing in 2026 (Tikketr, built with agency equ)? When did it switch, and from what (Red61 VIA?)? What does Red61 still power?
4. What is edfest.com? Who owns/operates it (company name, registration if findable), is it official or third-party, what does it sell (Fringe? Tattoo? International Festival?), and HOW does it sell - own inventory, agent agreement, affiliate links, white-label?
5. All the OTHER places a Fringe ticket is sold: venue-operator websites, on-street/venue box offices, the Half Price Hut, the official app, hotel concierges/agents, resale.
6. Verdict on the owner's premise: "edfringe.com and edfest.com are both just fronts for the event organizers themselves (Assembly, Gilded Balloon, Edinburgh Playhouse, etc.)". True, false, or what's the accurate model? Note: is Edinburgh Playhouse even a Fringe venue, or an ATG touring house?`,
  },
  {
    key: 'operators', verify: true,
    prompt: `${CTX}

TOPIC: The major Fringe venue operators ("event providers") and their own ticket channels.

For EACH of these operators, establish (a) that they operate venues at the 2026 Fringe and roughly how many spaces/venues, (b) their own box-office / ticket-selling URL if they have one, (c) the ticketing platform behind it if discoverable (Red61 VIA, Spektrix, TicketCo, Citizen Ticket, Ticketsolve, custom, or "sells only via edfringe"), each cited:
- Assembly Festival
- Pleasance (Edinburgh Festival)
- Underbelly
- Gilded Balloon
- Summerhall
- ZOO Venues
- C ARTS / C venues
- theSpaceUK
- Greenside Venues
- Just the Tonic
- Monkey Barrel Comedy
- The Stand Comedy Club
- Traverse Theatre
- Dance Base
- Scottish Storytelling Centre

Also answer: do the big operators sell tickets for their shows on their OWN sites in parallel with edfringe.com (same shows, two channels)? Any published statement of the split (what share the venue keeps vs allocates)? Historical context: the "Big Four" / "Comedy Festival" grouping (Assembly, Gilded Balloon, Pleasance, Underbelly) and whether they still jointly market (e.g. edcomfest / William Burdett-Coutts era). And: is Edinburgh Playhouse a Fringe venue in 2026 or an ATG receiving house outside the Fringe?`,
  },
  {
    key: 'free-tail', verify: true,
    prompt: `${CTX}

TOPIC: The free-show operators and the long tail of small venues - and how anyone can enumerate ALL providers.

Questions, each cited:
1. PBH's Free Fringe: how it works (unticketed, pass-the-bucket, no advance tickets?), where its listings live (own site/wee book?), scale in 2025/2026.
2. Laughing Horse Free Festival: same questions; do they do any advance/reserved tickets (e.g. via Eventbrite or their own site)?
3. Other mid-tier / independent operators active in 2025-2026: Sweet Venues, Paradise Green, Bedlam Theatre, Army @ The Fringe, Hill Street / Institut francais d'Ecosse, Quaker Meeting House, French Institute, Riddle's Court, any others you find. One line + citation each; skip any you cannot confirm for 2025/2026.
4. THE COMPLETENESS QUESTION: the Fringe is open-access - every show must register with the Fringe Society to be "in the Fringe", so the official programme/venue directory is by construction the exhaustive universe of providers. Confirm this open-access registration fact with citations (Fringe Society "how to take part" / venue registration guidance via search snippets). Is there a published venue directory or venue association (e.g. the Fringe Society's venue list, any "association of independent venues") a third party can enumerate?
5. Do free shows appear in edfringe.com's data like any other show (our scrape shows FREE_NON_TICKETED / FREE_TICKETED statuses - consistent with that)?`,
  },
  {
    key: 'selling', verify: true,
    prompt: `${CTX}

TOPIC: Can a third party SELL Fringe tickets itself - affiliate, agent, reseller, white-label, or ticketing API?

Questions, each cited; for each scheme found record: who offers it, what you get (deep links with commission? an actual purchase API? white-label storefront?), how to apply, cost/commission if published:
1. Does the Edinburgh Festival Fringe Society offer ANY of: an affiliate scheme, a ticket-agent programme, a white-label box office, a public ticketing/purchase API? Search hard: "edfringe affiliate", "fringe society ticket agents", "edfringe API", "fringe box office partner", artist/participant services pages, and their published ticketing terms & conditions. Also: do their T&Cs PROHIBIT unauthorized resale of Fringe tickets?
2. Do the big venue operators (Assembly, Pleasance, Underbelly, Gilded Balloon, Summerhall, Monkey Barrel, The Stand) run affiliate/agent programmes for their own box offices?
3. The ticketing PLATFORMS behind the box offices: Tikketr (equ), Red61 VIA, Spektrix, Citizen Ticket, TicketCo, Ticketsolve. Which of these offer partner/agent/affiliate APIs that would let a licensed third party sell a client's inventory? (Spektrix has a public API for a venue's own integrations; Red61 VIA has agent/API capabilities - verify and characterize.) The point: even where tech exists, does it require a COMMERCIAL AGREEMENT with each operator?
4. Are there existing third-party resellers/agents of Fringe tickets (TodayTix? Fever? hotel concierge programmes? edfest.com itself?) - evidence of what arrangement they have.
5. Realistic bottom line for a small independent site: what would "selling tickets ourselves" actually require, and what is the practical alternative (deep links to each seller, possibly with affiliate commission where schemes exist)?`,
