    prompt: `${CTX}

TOPIC: Listings DATA sources - could anything replace scraping edfringe.com?

We already know (from prior repo research): the official Edinburgh Festivals Listings API (api.edinburghfestivalcity.com, run by Festivals Edinburgh) is free with a registered key, covers all major festivals including the Fringe, exposes performances with start/end/price and venue lat/long, BUT has no per-performance availability/sold-out field, requires attribution, forbids redistributing dumps to third parties, and requires refreshing at least every 24h. Do NOT re-research those basics; go BEYOND them:
1. The Fringe-specific angle of that API: is its Fringe feed the same data as edfringe.com (it is the official listings database - who feeds it, how fresh is it during August, does it include Free Fringe / Free Festival shows)? Any published note on its Fringe update cadence or completeness vs edfringe.com?
2. Do any VENUE OPERATORS publish machine-readable listings: JSON endpoints, public APIs, iCal feeds on assemblyfestival.com, pleasance.co.uk, underbellyedinburgh.co.uk, gildedballoon.co.uk, summerhall, monkeybarrelcomedy.com, thestand.co.uk? Probe lightly with WebFetch where fetchable (e.g. look for /api/, wp-json, .json script tags mentioned in page source descriptions) and search for developer docs. Report honestly if nothing public exists.
3. Aggregators: Data Thistle events API (commercial - pricing/terms if findable), The List, Skiddle, Ents24, WeGotTickets, Eventbrite - do any carry substantial Fringe listings with an API?
4. Bottom line: for a product needing ALL ~3,650 shows with per-performance times AND ticket-availability status, rank the options: keep scraping edfringe GraphQL / official Listings API / per-operator feeds / aggregators. Which combination is actually viable and what does each lack?`,
  },
  {
    key: 'local-data', verify: false, schema: OPERATORS, effort: 'low',
    prompt: `You are analyzing a local dataset - NO web access needed; do not use WebSearch/WebFetch.

Repo: /home/user/EdFringeNow. File: data/normalized/shows.json - a JSON array of ~4,100 Edinburgh Fringe 2026 shows. Relevant fields per show: "venue" (venue code string), "venueName" (e.g. "Dexter at Underbelly, Bristo Square"), "free" (boolean).

TASK: produce the definitive operator (venue-family) breakdown of the 2026 programme.
1. Write a python3 script in the scratchpad dir (do NOT modify or add any repo files; read-only on the repo).
2. Extract distinct venues: map venue code -> venueName (one name per code; if a code has several names, keep the most common).
3. Group venues into OPERATOR FAMILIES by matching venueName against operator patterns (case-insensitive substring), checking longer/more-specific patterns first. Seed patterns (extend if you notice more families in the data): Assembly; Pleasance; Underbelly; Gilded Balloon; Summerhall; ZOO; "C ARTS" / "C venues" / "C aquila" / "C alto" (venueName often starts "C " or contains "C ARTS"); theSpace; Greenside; Just the Tonic; Monkey Barrel; "The Stand"; Laughing Horse; PBH / "Free Fringe"; Sweet; Paradise; Traverse; Dance Base; Scottish Storytelling; Bedlam; Army @ The Fringe; French Institute / Institut; Quaker; Hill Street; Riddle's Court; St Andrew Square / other.
4. For each family: number of distinct venue codes, number of shows, up to 5 example venueNames. Sort by show count descending.
5. Everything unmatched goes in unmatchedVenues as "venueName (N shows)" sorted by show count descending - list ALL of them, do not truncate to a top-N; this tail is the point of the exercise.
6. Also report: total shows, total distinct venue codes, count of shows with free=true.
7. In "method", describe the matching approach and its caveats (e.g. venueName substring matching can mis-file a venue whose name merely mentions an operator; note any you spotted and hand-fixed).
Sanity checks: totals must add up (sum of family shows + unmatched shows = total shows). Iterate on the patterns until the unmatched tail is genuinely small independents rather than obvious families.`,
  },
]

phase('Recon')
const results = await pipeline(
  RECON,
  (item) => agent(item.prompt, {
    label: `recon:${item.key}`,
    phase: 'Recon',
    schema: item.schema || FINDINGS,
    ...(item.effort ? { effort: item.effort } : {}),
  }),
  (recon, item) => {
    if (!recon || !item.verify) return { key: item.key, recon, verdicts: null }
    const claims = (recon.keyClaims || []).slice(0, 3)
    if (!claims.length) return { key: item.key, recon, verdicts: null }
    const claimList = claims.map((c, i) => `${i + 1}. ${c}`).join('\n')
    return agent(
      `${CTX}

You are an ADVERSARIAL fact-checker. A researcher investigating "${item.key}" for the Edinburgh Fringe ticketing landscape rests their conclusions on the claims below. Your job is to try to REFUTE each claim with your own independent web searches - do not reuse the researcher's sources as confirmation; find your own. A claim is "confirmed" only if independent evidence supports it; "plausible" if consistent with what you found but not independently confirmed; "refuted" if you found contrary evidence (then write the correction); "unverifiable" if the public record genuinely doesn't settle it. Be especially suspicious of: ownership claims (who runs/owns a website), "no such scheme exists" negatives (search for the scheme yourself before agreeing), and platform/vendor claims (which ticketing tech powers which site).

CLAIMS:
${claimList}`,
      { label: `verify:${item.key}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICTS },
    ).then((v) => ({ key: item.key, recon, verdicts: v ? v.verdicts : null }))
  },
)

const out = {}
for (const r of results.filter(Boolean)) out[r.key] = { recon: r.recon, verdicts: r.verdicts }
return out

content.content:
/* Partner (affiliate) links — the site's monetization plumbing.
 *
 * Pure: no DOM, no fetch. Each front-end renders the returned link itself
 * (the planner's schedule-board nags in plan/plan.js), and
 * shared/__tests__/affiliates.test.mjs exercises this module directly.
 *
 * Lives in shared/ rather than plan/ because a partner ID is a property of the
 * *site*, not of one page: the planner's "need a place to sleep?" nag and the
 * Now page's bookable-place pills both spend the same Booking.com ID, and both
 * pages must tag a click the same way or the referral is lost.
 *
 * Two things a caller needs:
 *   1. AFFILIATES — the ID block, empty until the programmes are joined.
 *   2. stayLink() / travelLink() — the two links the planner offers, each
 *      returning { text, partner, url } so the caller renders "text · partner".
 */

/* ---------- Monetization: affiliate / referral IDs ----------
 *
 * These are NOT secrets — they ship in the client, like the analytics token in
 * js/analytics.js — and they are empty by default: every link below works as a
 * plain deep link, and starts tagging referrals the moment an ID is filled in.
 * A partner whose IDs are unset simply stays untagged, so partial set-up is
 * fine (join accommodation first, transport later).
 *
 * TO MONETIZE, join the programmes and paste the IDs here. Which programme, at
 * what rate, and the sign-up checklist live in the tracking issue; the short
 * version (researched 2026-07-29):
 *   - Accommodation → Booking.com. `bookingAid` is the affiliate ID (Booking's
 *     own `aid` URL parameter), issued whether you join Booking.com's partner
 *     programme direct or through a network (Awin / CJ / Travelpayouts).
 *   - Transport → Omio (train + coach + flight in one search, which is what
 *     "how do I get to the Fringe?" actually needs). Omio's tracking is issued
 *     by whichever network accepts you, so it arrives as a *click wrapper*
 *     rather than a query parameter — see CLICK TEMPLATES below.
 */
export const AFFILIATES = {
  // Booking.com's `aid`, plus the `label` we send with it (see stayLink) so the
  // dashboard can tell this placement's earnings from any other.
  bookingAid: "",
  /* CLICK TEMPLATES — a network credits a click by routing it through its own
   * domain (awin1.com/cread.php?…&ued=, prf.hn/click/camref:…/destination:,
   * tp.media/click?…&deep_link=). The shapes differ per network and the real
   * one comes from the dashboard once accepted, so rather than guess a network
   * we take its link verbatim with `{deep}` marking where the destination URL
   * goes, URL-encoded. Empty template → the plain deep link, untagged. */
  bookingClickTemplate: "",
  omioClickTemplate: "",
};

/* Route a destination URL through a network's click wrapper. */
function wrapClick(template, url) {
  if (!template) return url;
  return template.replace("{deep}", encodeURIComponent(url));
}

/* The day after an ISO date, for the checkout of a stay that ends on the last
 * night. Pure calendar maths (UTC noon dodges DST edges), so month and year
 * boundaries just work. */
export function nextDayISO(dateISO) {
  const d = new Date(`${dateISO}T12:00:00Z`);
  d.setUTCDate(d.getUTCDate() + 1);
  return d.toISOString().slice(0, 10);
}

/* Somewhere to sleep for the nights of a Fringe trip: a Booking.com search for
 * Edinburgh with the trip's own dates pre-filled.
 *
 * `checkinISO`/`checkoutISO` are the planner's date window. A one-day window
 * would ask Booking.com for a zero-night stay (it returns nothing), so a
 * checkout that isn't after the check-in becomes the next morning — the honest
 * reading of "I'm here on the 12th and need a bed that night".
 */
export function stayLink({ checkinISO, checkoutISO } = {}, affiliates = AFFILIATES) {
  const params = new URLSearchParams({ ss: "Edinburgh" });
  if (checkinISO) {
    const checkout = checkoutISO && checkoutISO > checkinISO ? checkoutISO : nextDayISO(checkinISO);
    params.set("checkin", checkinISO);
    params.set("checkout", checkout);
  }
  if (affiliates.bookingAid) {
    params.set("aid", affiliates.bookingAid);
    // Booking.com's own per-link tag, so this nag's earnings are attributable.
    params.set("label", "edfringenow-plan-night");
  }
  const url = `https://www.booking.com/searchresults.html?${params}`;
  return {
    text: "Find a bed",
    partner: "Booking.com",
    url: wrapClick(affiliates.bookingClickTemplate, url),
  };
}

/* Getting to or out of Edinburgh. Omio's Edinburgh page covers train, coach and
 * flight in one search, which is the point: a last-minute Fringe-goer knows
 * they need to *get there*, not which mode is cheapest today.
 *
 * No origin and no date go on the URL. We don't know where the traveller starts
 * from (the planner only ever knows Edinburgh), and Omio's route pages key off
 * an origin slug, so a guessed one would 404 where the destination page always
 * works.
 */
export function travelLink(affiliates = AFFILIATES) {
  const url = "https://www.omio.com/travel/edinburgh";
  return {
    text: "Trains, coaches & flights",
    partner: "Omio",
    url: wrapClick(affiliates.omioClickTemplate, url),
  };
}

